/*****************************************************************************
    bodyT -- THE ALIASING EXHIBIT, and the check that certifies the mint fix
    when it lands. 2026-08-17.

    ⚠ WHAT IT DEMONSTRATES, measured this session: two rules given a generated
    CodE attribute the reuse-a-shared-name way end up sharing ONE groupBody, so
    the second generation OVERWRITES the first. Bodies are correct at the moment
    they print and are aliases by the time anything reads them back.

    THE MECHANISM, named precisely because it is a family and not a one-off:
    a bare name in an action body reaches THE SEARCH LIST before any local
    scope, so `CodE` is not a local -- it resolves to the language's own
    persistent CodE node. Measured: the same node address across three separate
    calls of a probe action, with no assignment between them. So an assignment
    to it writes through a resolved name into a shared persistent node, once per
    call, forever. Name-scope pollution, the same class as bear-trap 26's
    plausible-name-resolves-somewhere.

    ⚠ AND `+%` IS INNOCENT -- ALSO MEASURED. It SHARES the body rather than
    copying it, which is correct behaviour and was confirmed by pointer: the
    local and the attached node have different node addresses and identical
    groupBody addresses. Sharing does exactly what sharing does; the defect is
    that what gets attached was never a fresh body per rule.

    THE FIX DIRECTION, settled by those two measurements together: MINT per
    rule, do not reuse a shared name. new on the tag, then fill, then attach.
    With a fresh body per rule the alias class is unconstructable rather than
    avoided.

    ⚠ HOW TO READ THIS FIXTURE AFTER THE MINT LANDS -- ITS VERDICT INVERTS,
    DELIBERATELY, AND THAT IS THE POINT:
        BEFORE the fix  the two groupBody addresses MATCH and both hosts read
                        the second content. That is the defect, exhibited.
        AFTER  the fix  the two addresses DIFFER and each host reads its own
                        content. That is the certification.
    So do not "repair" this fixture when it starts reporting the other thing.
    Update the expectation and say which state was measured, per H6.

    The pointer rows come from showBody, which exists for exactly this: incant
    accessors are snapshot-by-value, so two probes returning the same text prove
    text equality and never node identity.
*****************************************************************************/

register(BodySubj);
define
    btBuf buffer;
    btHostA;
    btHostB;
    btProbe code={
        reset(btBuf);
        printTO(btBuf);
        print "AAAA":;
        printTO(0);
        CodE = btBuf;
        btHostA +% CodE;

        reset(btBuf);
        printTO(btBuf);
        print "BBBB":;
        printTO(0);
        CodE = btBuf;
        btHostB +% CodE;

        ga := btHostA["CodE"];
        gb := btHostB["CodE"];
        showBody(ga);
        showBody(gb);
        cerr "hostA CodE reads:" ga:;
        cerr "hostB CodE reads:" gb:;
        cerr "  MATCHING groupBody addresses above = ALIASED (the defect)":;
        cerr "  DIFFERING addresses + AAAA/BBBB    = minted (the fix)":;
        };
    ;

btProbe();
cerr "BODYT SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
